BT /F2 9.7 Tf 0.16 0.18 0.23 rg 1 0 0 1 48.0 745.0 Tm (10. A pressure tracing shows atrial contraction shortly after an ECG P wave and ventricular contraction) Tj ET
BT /F2 9.7 Tf 0.16 0.18 0.23 rg 1 0 0 1 48.0 731.3 Tm (shortly after QRS. What principle explains both delays?) Tj ET
BT /F2 9.3 Tf 0.1 0.33 0.58 rg 1 0 0 1 48.0 714.6 Tm (Answer: A. Electrical excitation normally precedes the mechanical response of cardiac muscle.) Tj ET
BT /F1 9.1 Tf 0.16 0.18 0.23 rg 1 0 0 1 48.0 698.3 Tm (Explanation: The electrical depolarisation that activates cardiac muscle precedes its force development. P) Tj ET
BT /F1 9.1 Tf 0.16 0.18 0.23 rg 1 0 0 1 48.0 685.2 Tm (precedes atrial systole, and QRS precedes ventricular systole.) Tj ET
BT /F1 8.4 Tf 0.16 0.18 0.23 rg 1 0 0 1 48.0 669.1 Tm (Not B: This reverses the normal electrical-to-mechanical sequence.) Tj ET
BT /F1 8.4 Tf 0.16 0.18 0.23 rg 1 0 0 1 48.0 655.7 Tm (Not C: The T wave reflects ventricular electrical recovery, after ventricular depolarisation.) Tj ET
BT /F1 8.4 Tf 0.16 0.18 0.23 rg 1 0 0 1 48.0 642.3 Tm (Not D: Pressure changes follow electrical activation and are not what skin ECG leads measure.) Tj ET
BT /F1 7.2 Tf 0.43 0.45 0.49 rg 1 0 0 1 48.0 628.9 Tm (Source: lecture-7:Q34 | 6-11; Chapter 6 ?5) Tj ET
