BT /F1 8.0 Tf 1 1 1 rg 1 0 0 1 48.0 798.0 Tm (MOCK 02 | 40Q | EXPLANATION GUIDE | 60 MIN) Tj ET
BT /F2 9.7 Tf 0.16 0.18 0.23 rg 1 0 0 1 48.0 745.0 Tm (15. A resting muscle suddenly becomes active while systemic arterial pressure stays steady. What most) Tj ET
BT /F2 9.7 Tf 0.16 0.18 0.23 rg 1 0 0 1 48.0 731.3 Tm (directly increases its blood supply?) Tj ET
BT /F2 9.3 Tf 0.1 0.33 0.58 rg 1 0 0 1 48.0 714.6 Tm (Answer: C. Its local arterioles reduce their resistance.) Tj ET
BT /F1 9.1 Tf 0.16 0.18 0.23 rg 1 0 0 1 48.0 698.3 Tm (Explanation: Flow = pressure difference / resistance. With arterial pressure unchanged, the only way to get more) Tj ET
BT /F1 9.1 Tf 0.16 0.18 0.23 rg 1 0 0 1 48.0 685.2 Tm (flow is lower resistance, and the resistance that tissues control is in their arterioles. Metabolites from the) Tj ET
BT /F1 9.1 Tf 0.16 0.18 0.23 rg 1 0 0 1 48.0 672.1 Tm (working muscle relax those arterioles.) Tj ET
BT /F1 8.4 Tf 0.16 0.18 0.23 rg 1 0 0 1 48.0 656.0 Tm (Not A: Vein compliance mainly affects stored volume, not the main resistance to flow.) Tj ET
BT /F1 8.4 Tf 0.16 0.18 0.23 rg 1 0 0 1 48.0 642.6 Tm (Not B: The stem states that systemic arterial pressure remains steady.) Tj ET
BT /F1 8.4 Tf 0.16 0.18 0.23 rg 1 0 0 1 48.0 629.2 Tm (Not D: Capillaries lack the smooth muscle needed for this response.) Tj ET
BT /F1 7.2 Tf 0.43 0.45 0.49 rg 1 0 0 1 48.0 615.8 Tm (Source: circulation-2:Q1 | 1-4) Tj ET
BT /F2 9.7 Tf 0.16 0.18 0.23 rg 1 0 0 1 48.0 592.6 Tm (16. At one instant, Lead I records +0.4 mV and Lead III records -0.2 mV. What must Lead II record?) Tj ET
BT /F2 9.3 Tf 0.1 0.33 0.58 rg 1 0 0 1 48.0 575.9 Tm (Answer: C. +0.2 mV, from adding the signed voltages.) Tj ET
BT /F1 9.1 Tf 0.16 0.18 0.23 rg 1 0 0 1 48.0 559.6 Tm (Explanation: Einthoven's law is V\(II\) = V\(I\) + V\(III\). Therefore +0.4 + \(-0.2\) = +0.2 mV at the same instant.) Tj ET
BT /F1 8.4 Tf 0.16 0.18 0.23 rg 1 0 0 1 48.0 543.5 Tm (Not A: Lead II is obtained by combining the two measured limb-lead voltages.) Tj ET
BT /F1 8.4 Tf 0.16 0.18 0.23 rg 1 0 0 1 48.0 530.1 Tm (Not B: The negative sign of Lead III must be retained in the sum.) Tj ET
BT /F1 8.4 Tf 0.16 0.18 0.23 rg 1 0 0 1 48.0 516.7 Tm (Not D: This reverses the sign of the result rather than adding the signed voltages.) Tj ET
BT /F1 7.2 Tf 0.43 0.45 0.49 rg 1 0 0 1 48.0 503.3 Tm (Source: lecture-7:Q8 | 3; Chapter 6 ?3) Tj ET
BT /F2 9.7 Tf 0.16 0.18 0.23 rg 1 0 0 1 48.0 480.1 Tm (17. A region of heart muscle starts working harder. Which local product of its own metabolism helps increase) Tj ET
BT /F2 9.7 Tf 0.16 0.18 0.23 rg 1 0 0 1 48.0 466.4 Tm (its blood flow?) Tj ET
BT /F2 9.3 Tf 0.1 0.33 0.58 rg 1 0 0 1 48.0 449.7 Tm (Answer: D. Adenosine formed as ATP is used up.) Tj ET
BT /F1 9.1 Tf 0.16 0.18 0.23 rg 1 0 0 1 48.0 433.4 Tm (Explanation: Working muscle uses ATP faster, and its breakdown products \(adenosine, ADP, AMP\) build up and dilate) Tj ET
BT /F1 9.1 Tf 0.16 0.18 0.23 rg 1 0 0 1 48.0 420.3 Tm (the local arterioles. Flow rises in proportion to how hard the tissue works, which is why the heart relies so heavily) Tj ET
BT /F1 9.1 Tf 0.16 0.18 0.23 rg 1 0 0 1 48.0 407.2 Tm (on this.) Tj ET
BT /F1 8.4 Tf 0.16 0.18 0.23 rg 1 0 0 1 48.0 391.1 Tm (Not A: Platelet serotonin constricts vessels, and it comes from platelets, not from the working muscle.) Tj ET
BT /F1 8.4 Tf 0.16 0.18 0.23 rg 1 0 0 1 48.0 377.7 Tm (Not B: Angiotensin II is a circulating constrictor, not a product of the muscle's metabolism.) Tj ET
BT /F1 8.4 Tf 0.16 0.18 0.23 rg 1 0 0 1 48.0 364.3 Tm (Not C: Noradrenaline is a nerve transmitter that mainly constricts arterioles via alpha1 receptors.) Tj ET
BT /F1 7.2 Tf 0.43 0.45 0.49 rg 1 0 0 1 48.0 350.9 Tm (Source: circulation-2:Q9 | 6) Tj ET
BT /F2 9.7 Tf 0.16 0.18 0.23 rg 1 0 0 1 48.0 327.7 Tm (18. What physiological purpose is served by slow conduction through the AV node?) Tj ET
BT /F2 9.3 Tf 0.1 0.33 0.58 rg 1 0 0 1 48.0 311.0 Tm (Answer: D. It allows atrial contraction to finish ventricular filling.) Tj ET
BT /F1 9.1 Tf 0.16 0.18 0.23 rg 1 0 0 1 48.0 294.7 Tm (Explanation: The AV delay separates atrial and ventricular activation so atrial systole can augment ventricular) Tj ET
BT /F1 9.1 Tf 0.16 0.18 0.23 rg 1 0 0 1 48.0 281.6 Tm (filling before ventricular systole.) Tj ET
BT /F1 8.4 Tf 0.16 0.18 0.23 rg 1 0 0 1 48.0 265.5 Tm (Not A: Normal impulses subsequently travel through His and Purkinje fibres.) Tj ET
BT /F1 8.4 Tf 0.16 0.18 0.23 rg 1 0 0 1 48.0 252.1 Tm (Not B: The sequence is atrial activation before ventricular activation.) Tj ET
BT /F1 8.4 Tf 0.16 0.18 0.23 rg 1 0 0 1 48.0 238.7 Tm (Not C: The aortic valve remains closed during ordinary atrial systole.) Tj ET
BT /F1 7.2 Tf 0.43 0.45 0.49 rg 1 0 0 1 48.0 225.3 Tm (Source: circulation-1:Q14 | 8-10; Chapter 3 ?5) Tj ET
BT /F2 9.7 Tf 0.16 0.18 0.23 rg 1 0 0 1 48.0 202.1 Tm (19. Which statement best reflects the updated interpretation of capillary fluid balance?) Tj ET
BT /F2 9.3 Tf 0.1 0.33 0.58 rg 1 0 0 1 48.0 185.4 Tm (Answer: A. The glycocalyx regulates filtration; lymph returns excess fluid.) Tj ET
BT /F1 9.1 Tf 0.16 0.18 0.23 rg 1 0 0 1 48.0 169.1 Tm (Explanation: The revised Starling model includes the glycocalyx and emphasises that sustained net filtration is) Tj ET
BT /F1 9.1 Tf 0.16 0.18 0.23 rg 1 0 0 1 48.0 156.0 Tm (normally cleared by lymphatics.) Tj ET
BT /F1 8.4 Tf 0.16 0.18 0.23 rg 1 0 0 1 48.0 139.9 Tm (Not B: Lymphatic return remains vital even at normal pressures.) Tj ET
BT /F1 8.4 Tf 0.16 0.18 0.23 rg 1 0 0 1 48.0 126.5 Tm (Not C: The classical arterial filtration-venous reabsorption picture overstates sustained reabsorption.) Tj ET
BT /F1 8.4 Tf 0.16 0.18 0.23 rg 1 0 0 1 48.0 113.1 Tm (Not D: Protein distributions can influence effective oncotic gradients.) Tj ET
BT /F1 7.2 Tf 0.43 0.45 0.49 rg 1 0 0 1 48.0 99.7 Tm (Source: circulation-1:Q84 | 85-88; Chapter 13 ?6 \(modern clarification\)) Tj ET
