BT /F2 9.7 Tf 0.16 0.18 0.23 rg 1 0 0 1 48.0 745.0 Tm (25. An ECG machine runs at 25 mm/s. How much time passes while the paper advances by 1 mm horizontally?) Tj ET
BT /F2 9.3 Tf 0.1 0.33 0.58 rg 1 0 0 1 48.0 728.3 Tm (Answer: D. 0.04 s.) Tj ET
BT /F1 9.1 Tf 0.16 0.18 0.23 rg 1 0 0 1 48.0 712.0 Tm (Explanation: At a speed of 25 mm each second, 1 mm represents 1/25 s = 0.04 s, the duration of one small horizontal) Tj ET
BT /F1 9.1 Tf 0.16 0.18 0.23 rg 1 0 0 1 48.0 698.9 Tm (grid square.) Tj ET
BT /F1 8.4 Tf 0.16 0.18 0.23 rg 1 0 0 1 48.0 682.8 Tm (Not A: 0.10 s does not correspond to a 1-mm horizontal square at this speed.) Tj ET
BT /F1 8.4 Tf 0.16 0.18 0.23 rg 1 0 0 1 48.0 669.4 Tm (Not B: 0.50 s would occupy 12.5 small squares.) Tj ET
BT /F1 8.4 Tf 0.16 0.18 0.23 rg 1 0 0 1 48.0 656.0 Tm (Not C: 0.20 s is the time represented by one 5-mm large square.) Tj ET
BT /F1 7.2 Tf 0.43 0.45 0.49 rg 1 0 0 1 48.0 642.6 Tm (Source: lecture-7:Q35 | 2, 13) Tj ET
BT /F2 9.7 Tf 0.16 0.18 0.23 rg 1 0 0 1 48.0 619.4 Tm (26. A cerebral vessel ruptures and blood enters brain tissue. Which type of cerebrovascular event is present?) Tj ET
BT /F2 9.3 Tf 0.1 0.33 0.58 rg 1 0 0 1 48.0 602.7 Tm (Answer: D. Haemorrhagic stroke.) Tj ET
BT /F1 9.1 Tf 0.16 0.18 0.23 rg 1 0 0 1 48.0 586.4 Tm (Explanation: Haemorrhagic stroke results from rupture of a vessel, whereas ischaemic stroke results from) Tj ET
BT /F1 9.1 Tf 0.16 0.18 0.23 rg 1 0 0 1 48.0 573.3 Tm (interrupted arterial supply.) Tj ET
BT /F1 8.4 Tf 0.16 0.18 0.23 rg 1 0 0 1 48.0 557.2 Tm (Not A: Hypoglycaemia causes global dysfunction rather than vessel rupture.) Tj ET
BT /F1 8.4 Tf 0.16 0.18 0.23 rg 1 0 0 1 48.0 543.8 Tm (Not B: Embolism blocks flow rather than directly representing vessel rupture.) Tj ET
BT /F1 8.4 Tf 0.16 0.18 0.23 rg 1 0 0 1 48.0 530.4 Tm (Not C: Thrombosis obstructs an artery instead of rupturing it.) Tj ET
BT /F1 7.2 Tf 0.43 0.45 0.49 rg 1 0 0 1 48.0 517.0 Tm (Source: lecture-6:Q18 | 25) Tj ET
BT /F2 9.7 Tf 0.16 0.18 0.23 rg 1 0 0 1 48.0 493.8 Tm (27. A ventricle has an end-diastolic volume of 130 mL and an end-systolic volume of 55 mL. What is its stroke) Tj ET
BT /F2 9.7 Tf 0.16 0.18 0.23 rg 1 0 0 1 48.0 480.1 Tm (volume?) Tj ET
BT /F2 9.3 Tf 0.1 0.33 0.58 rg 1 0 0 1 48.0 463.4 Tm (Answer: B. 75 mL.) Tj ET
BT /F1 9.1 Tf 0.16 0.18 0.23 rg 1 0 0 1 48.0 447.1 Tm (Explanation: Stroke volume is EDV minus ESV, so 130 - 55 = 75 mL per beat.) Tj ET
BT /F1 8.4 Tf 0.16 0.18 0.23 rg 1 0 0 1 48.0 431.0 Tm (Not A: One hundred thirty mL is end-diastolic volume.) Tj ET
BT /F1 8.4 Tf 0.16 0.18 0.23 rg 1 0 0 1 48.0 417.6 Tm (Not C: Fifty-five mL is end-systolic volume.) Tj ET
BT /F1 8.4 Tf 0.16 0.18 0.23 rg 1 0 0 1 48.0 404.2 Tm (Not D: Adding EDV and ESV does not give stroke volume.) Tj ET
BT /F1 7.2 Tf 0.43 0.45 0.49 rg 1 0 0 1 48.0 390.8 Tm (Source: circulation-1:Q22 | 16; Chapter 4 ?7) Tj ET
BT /F2 9.7 Tf 0.16 0.18 0.23 rg 1 0 0 1 48.0 367.6 Tm (28. How does CSF substantially reduce the effective weight of the brain?) Tj ET
BT /F2 9.3 Tf 0.1 0.33 0.58 rg 1 0 0 1 48.0 350.9 Tm (Answer: B. It provides buoyant support against gravity.) Tj ET
BT /F1 9.1 Tf 0.16 0.18 0.23 rg 1 0 0 1 48.0 334.6 Tm (Explanation: Immersion provides buoyancy, reducing the brain's effective supported weight from roughly 1400-1500 g) Tj ET
BT /F1 9.1 Tf 0.16 0.18 0.23 rg 1 0 0 1 48.0 321.5 Tm (to around 50 g.) Tj ET
BT /F1 8.4 Tf 0.16 0.18 0.23 rg 1 0 0 1 48.0 305.4 Tm (Not A: The tissue density does not increase to create buoyancy.) Tj ET
BT /F1 8.4 Tf 0.16 0.18 0.23 rg 1 0 0 1 48.0 292.0 Tm (Not C: Brain blood volume remains substantial.) Tj ET
BT /F1 8.4 Tf 0.16 0.18 0.23 rg 1 0 0 1 48.0 278.6 Tm (Not D: Lower oxygen consumption does not explain mechanical support.) Tj ET
BT /F1 7.2 Tf 0.43 0.45 0.49 rg 1 0 0 1 48.0 265.2 Tm (Source: lecture-6:Q9 | 13-15) Tj ET
BT /F2 9.7 Tf 0.16 0.18 0.23 rg 1 0 0 1 48.0 242.0 Tm (29. Why is 1500 used to calculate rate from the number of small squares between R waves at 25 mm/s?) Tj ET
BT /F2 9.3 Tf 0.1 0.33 0.58 rg 1 0 0 1 48.0 225.3 Tm (Answer: A. It is the number of 1-mm squares passing in one minute.) Tj ET
BT /F1 9.1 Tf 0.16 0.18 0.23 rg 1 0 0 1 48.0 209.0 Tm (Explanation: At 25 mm/s, the paper travels 25 x 60 = 1500 mm per minute. Dividing these 1500 small squares by squares) Tj ET
BT /F1 9.1 Tf 0.16 0.18 0.23 rg 1 0 0 1 48.0 195.9 Tm (per beat yields beats per minute.) Tj ET
BT /F1 8.4 Tf 0.16 0.18 0.23 rg 1 0 0 1 48.0 179.8 Tm (Not B: The distance travelled per cycle varies with RR duration.) Tj ET
BT /F1 8.4 Tf 0.16 0.18 0.23 rg 1 0 0 1 48.0 166.4 Tm (Not C: Vertical voltage is calibrated at 10 mm per mV.) Tj ET
BT /F1 8.4 Tf 0.16 0.18 0.23 rg 1 0 0 1 48.0 153.0 Tm (Not D: A person's actual heart rate is not fixed at 1500 beats per minute.) Tj ET
BT /F1 7.2 Tf 0.43 0.45 0.49 rg 1 0 0 1 48.0 139.6 Tm (Source: lecture-7:Q44 | 2, 13-14) Tj ET
