BT /F1 9.1 Tf 0.16 0.18 0.23 rg 1 0 0 1 48.0 559.0 Tm (Explanation: Sympathetic arteriolar constriction can maintain arterial pressure despite reduced cardiac output and) Tj ET
BT /F1 9.1 Tf 0.16 0.18 0.23 rg 1 0 0 1 48.0 545.9 Tm (poor perfusion of some tissues.) Tj ET
BT /F1 8.4 Tf 0.16 0.18 0.23 rg 1 0 0 1 48.0 529.8 Tm (Not A: Shock is defined by inadequate tissue perfusion, not one pressure value.) Tj ET
BT /F1 8.4 Tf 0.16 0.18 0.23 rg 1 0 0 1 48.0 516.4 Tm (Not C: Haemorrhage reduces venous return and usually lowers output.) Tj ET
BT /F1 8.4 Tf 0.16 0.18 0.23 rg 1 0 0 1 48.0 503.0 Tm (Not D: Flow can be redistributed away from skin and kidneys.) Tj ET
BT /F1 7.2 Tf 0.43 0.45 0.49 rg 1 0 0 1 48.0 489.6 Tm (Source: circulation-3:Q18 | Circulation 3, slides 12-13; Chapter 20 ?1) Tj ET
BT /F2 9.7 Tf 0.16 0.18 0.23 rg 1 0 0 1 48.0 466.4 Tm (32. Why can chronic widespread alveolar hypoxia lead to pulmonary hypertension?) Tj ET
BT /F2 9.3 Tf 0.1 0.33 0.58 rg 1 0 0 1 48.0 449.7 Tm (Answer: B. Persistent hypoxic constriction increases lung resistance.) Tj ET
BT /F1 9.1 Tf 0.16 0.18 0.23 rg 1 0 0 1 48.0 433.4 Tm (Explanation: When hypoxic constriction affects much of the lung, cumulative resistance rises and pulmonary arterial) Tj ET
BT /F1 9.1 Tf 0.16 0.18 0.23 rg 1 0 0 1 48.0 420.3 Tm (pressure can increase.) Tj ET
BT /F1 8.4 Tf 0.16 0.18 0.23 rg 1 0 0 1 48.0 404.2 Tm (Not A: Recruitment is a different response that lowers resistance.) Tj ET
BT /F1 8.4 Tf 0.16 0.18 0.23 rg 1 0 0 1 48.0 390.8 Tm (Not C: The lecture explicitly associates hypoxia with pulmonary vasoconstriction.) Tj ET
BT /F1 8.4 Tf 0.16 0.18 0.23 rg 1 0 0 1 48.0 377.4 Tm (Not D: Lung flow generally continues but faces increased resistance.) Tj ET
BT /F1 7.2 Tf 0.43 0.45 0.49 rg 1 0 0 1 48.0 364.0 Tm (Source: lecture-6:Q36 | 32) Tj ET
BT /F2 9.7 Tf 0.16 0.18 0.23 rg 1 0 0 1 48.0 340.8 Tm (33. Once local metabolic dilation restores an organ's blood flow, what prevents dilation from continuing) Tj ET
BT /F2 9.7 Tf 0.16 0.18 0.23 rg 1 0 0 1 48.0 327.1 Tm (unchecked?) Tj ET
BT /F2 9.3 Tf 0.1 0.33 0.58 rg 1 0 0 1 48.0 310.4 Tm (Answer: C. The restored flow clears accumulated dilator metabolites.) Tj ET
BT /F1 9.1 Tf 0.16 0.18 0.23 rg 1 0 0 1 48.0 294.1 Tm (Explanation: The dilators exist because flow was too low for demand. Once flow matches demand, they are washed away) Tj ET
BT /F1 9.1 Tf 0.16 0.18 0.23 rg 1 0 0 1 48.0 281.0 Tm (as fast as they are made, so the arterioles regain their tone. This negative feedback stops dilation running away.) Tj ET
BT /F1 8.4 Tf 0.16 0.18 0.23 rg 1 0 0 1 48.0 264.9 Tm (Not A: Restored delivery generally improves tissue oxygen availability.) Tj ET
BT /F1 8.4 Tf 0.16 0.18 0.23 rg 1 0 0 1 48.0 251.5 Tm (Not B: Sustained lactate accumulation would favour continued dilation.) Tj ET
BT /F1 8.4 Tf 0.16 0.18 0.23 rg 1 0 0 1 48.0 238.1 Tm (Not D: Vascular smooth muscle remains present and functional.) Tj ET
BT /F1 7.2 Tf 0.43 0.45 0.49 rg 1 0 0 1 48.0 224.7 Tm (Source: circulation-2:Q10 | 5-6) Tj ET
BT /F2 9.7 Tf 0.16 0.18 0.23 rg 1 0 0 1 48.0 201.5 Tm (34. A graph of organ flow against perfusion pressure is almost flat over a middle range but slopes downward at) Tj ET
BT /F2 9.7 Tf 0.16 0.18 0.23 rg 1 0 0 1 48.0 187.8 Tm (very low pressures. Why?) Tj ET
BT /F2 9.3 Tf 0.1 0.33 0.58 rg 1 0 0 1 48.0 171.1 Tm (Answer: A. The arterioles reach their maximal dilation at low pressure.) Tj ET
BT /F1 9.1 Tf 0.16 0.18 0.23 rg 1 0 0 1 48.0 154.8 Tm (Explanation: Once arterioles are fully dilated, they cannot reduce resistance further, so flow decreases as) Tj ET
BT /F1 9.1 Tf 0.16 0.18 0.23 rg 1 0 0 1 48.0 141.7 Tm (pressure falls.) Tj ET
BT /F1 8.4 Tf 0.16 0.18 0.23 rg 1 0 0 1 48.0 125.6 Tm (Not B: Maximal constriction occurs in response to rising pressure, not very low pressure.) Tj ET
BT /F1 8.4 Tf 0.16 0.18 0.23 rg 1 0 0 1 48.0 112.2 Tm (Not C: A downward slope does not mean perfusion pressure is zero.) Tj ET
BT /F1 8.4 Tf 0.16 0.18 0.23 rg 1 0 0 1 48.0 98.8 Tm (Not D: Venous vessels do not replace arterioles as the chief resistance site.) Tj ET
BT /F1 7.2 Tf 0.43 0.45 0.49 rg 1 0 0 1 48.0 85.4 Tm (Source: circulation-2:Q4 | 4-5, 8-11) Tj ET
