BT /F2 9.7 Tf 0.16 0.18 0.23 rg 1 0 0 1 48.0 745.0 Tm (35. A burn patient presents with shock and an initially high haematocrit. What explains the contrast with) Tj ET
BT /F2 9.7 Tf 0.16 0.18 0.23 rg 1 0 0 1 48.0 731.3 Tm (recent whole-blood haemorrhage?) Tj ET
BT /F2 9.3 Tf 0.1 0.33 0.58 rg 1 0 0 1 48.0 714.6 Tm (Answer: D. Plasma leakage leaves intravascular red cells concentrated.) Tj ET
BT /F1 9.1 Tf 0.16 0.18 0.23 rg 1 0 0 1 48.0 698.3 Tm (Explanation: Severe burns can cause plasma to leak through damaged skin while red cells remain, producing) Tj ET
BT /F1 9.1 Tf 0.16 0.18 0.23 rg 1 0 0 1 48.0 685.2 Tm (haemoconcentration and raised haematocrit.) Tj ET
BT /F1 8.4 Tf 0.16 0.18 0.23 rg 1 0 0 1 48.0 669.1 Tm (Not A: Selective red-cell destruction would tend to lower red-cell concentration.) Tj ET
BT /F1 8.4 Tf 0.16 0.18 0.23 rg 1 0 0 1 48.0 655.7 Tm (Not B: Plasma loss reduces rather than increases effective circulating volume.) Tj ET
BT /F1 8.4 Tf 0.16 0.18 0.23 rg 1 0 0 1 48.0 642.3 Tm (Not C: Erythropoiesis is much too slow to explain this early result.) Tj ET
BT /F1 7.2 Tf 0.43 0.45 0.49 rg 1 0 0 1 48.0 628.9 Tm (Source: circulation-3:Q43 | Circulation 3, slides 28) Tj ET
BT /F2 9.7 Tf 0.16 0.18 0.23 rg 1 0 0 1 48.0 605.7 Tm (36. The jugular venous 'c' wave is chiefly linked to which event?) Tj ET
BT /F2 9.3 Tf 0.1 0.33 0.58 rg 1 0 0 1 48.0 589.0 Tm (Answer: C. Systolic bulging of the tricuspid valve.) Tj ET
BT /F1 9.1 Tf 0.16 0.18 0.23 rg 1 0 0 1 48.0 572.7 Tm (Explanation: Early right ventricular contraction can bulge the shut tricuspid valve toward the right atrium,) Tj ET
BT /F1 9.1 Tf 0.16 0.18 0.23 rg 1 0 0 1 48.0 559.6 Tm (contributing to the c wave.) Tj ET
BT /F1 8.4 Tf 0.16 0.18 0.23 rg 1 0 0 1 48.0 543.5 Tm (Not A: Rapid atrial emptying contributes to the y descent.) Tj ET
BT /F1 8.4 Tf 0.16 0.18 0.23 rg 1 0 0 1 48.0 530.1 Tm (Not B: Venous filling behind a closed tricuspid valve makes the v wave.) Tj ET
BT /F1 8.4 Tf 0.16 0.18 0.23 rg 1 0 0 1 48.0 516.7 Tm (Not D: Right atrial systole generates the a wave.) Tj ET
BT /F1 7.2 Tf 0.43 0.45 0.49 rg 1 0 0 1 48.0 503.3 Tm (Source: circulation-1:Q68 | 15, 24-25; Chapter 11 ?4) Tj ET
BT /F2 9.7 Tf 0.16 0.18 0.23 rg 1 0 0 1 48.0 480.1 Tm (37. When both atrioventricular valves are open and semilunar valves are shut, which process predominates?) Tj ET
BT /F2 9.3 Tf 0.1 0.33 0.58 rg 1 0 0 1 48.0 463.4 Tm (Answer: B. Ventricular filling during diastole.) Tj ET
BT /F1 9.1 Tf 0.16 0.18 0.23 rg 1 0 0 1 48.0 447.1 Tm (Explanation: With ventricular pressure below atrial pressure, AV valves open and blood enters relaxed ventricles) Tj ET
BT /F1 9.1 Tf 0.16 0.18 0.23 rg 1 0 0 1 48.0 434.0 Tm (while semilunar valves remain closed.) Tj ET
BT /F1 8.4 Tf 0.16 0.18 0.23 rg 1 0 0 1 48.0 417.9 Tm (Not A: Ejection requires open semilunar valves.) Tj ET
BT /F1 8.4 Tf 0.16 0.18 0.23 rg 1 0 0 1 48.0 404.5 Tm (Not C: Outflow valve closure happens near the start of ventricular relaxation.) Tj ET
BT /F1 8.4 Tf 0.16 0.18 0.23 rg 1 0 0 1 48.0 391.1 Tm (Not D: Isovolumetric contraction occurs after AV valves close.) Tj ET
BT /F1 7.2 Tf 0.43 0.45 0.49 rg 1 0 0 1 48.0 377.7 Tm (Source: circulation-1:Q3 | 11-13) Tj ET
BT /F2 9.7 Tf 0.16 0.18 0.23 rg 1 0 0 1 48.0 354.5 Tm (38. Following regional node removal, a patient develops persistent firm limb swelling. What explains it?) Tj ET
BT /F2 9.3 Tf 0.1 0.33 0.58 rg 1 0 0 1 48.0 337.8 Tm (Answer: C. Protein accumulation after failed lymph drainage.) Tj ET
BT /F1 9.1 Tf 0.16 0.18 0.23 rg 1 0 0 1 48.0 321.5 Tm (Explanation: Lymphatic obstruction impairs removal of interstitial proteins and fluid, leading over time to) Tj ET
BT /F1 9.1 Tf 0.16 0.18 0.23 rg 1 0 0 1 48.0 308.4 Tm (persistent, often non-pitting swelling.) Tj ET
BT /F1 8.4 Tf 0.16 0.18 0.23 rg 1 0 0 1 48.0 292.3 Tm (Not A: Oxygen delivery is not the main mechanism.) Tj ET
BT /F1 8.4 Tf 0.16 0.18 0.23 rg 1 0 0 1 48.0 278.9 Tm (Not B: Cardiac ejection fraction need not be abnormal.) Tj ET
BT /F1 8.4 Tf 0.16 0.18 0.23 rg 1 0 0 1 48.0 265.5 Tm (Not D: Impaired lymph drainage prevents, rather than accelerates, interstitial protein removal.) Tj ET
BT /F1 7.2 Tf 0.43 0.45 0.49 rg 1 0 0 1 48.0 252.1 Tm (Source: circulation-1:Q90 | 94-95) Tj ET
BT /F2 9.7 Tf 0.16 0.18 0.23 rg 1 0 0 1 48.0 228.9 Tm (39. A blood-pressure reading is 132/84 mmHg. What is the pulse pressure?) Tj ET
BT /F2 9.3 Tf 0.1 0.33 0.58 rg 1 0 0 1 48.0 212.2 Tm (Answer: C. 48 mmHg.) Tj ET
BT /F1 9.1 Tf 0.16 0.18 0.23 rg 1 0 0 1 48.0 195.9 Tm (Explanation: Pulse pressure equals systolic minus diastolic: 132 - 84 = 48 mmHg. It measures the pressure swing) Tj ET
BT /F1 9.1 Tf 0.16 0.18 0.23 rg 1 0 0 1 48.0 182.8 Tm (between systole and diastole.) Tj ET
BT /F1 8.4 Tf 0.16 0.18 0.23 rg 1 0 0 1 48.0 166.7 Tm (Not A: Thirty-six is below the arithmetic difference.) Tj ET
BT /F1 8.4 Tf 0.16 0.18 0.23 rg 1 0 0 1 48.0 153.3 Tm (Not B: Forty-four is four mmHg below the difference.) Tj ET
BT /F1 8.4 Tf 0.16 0.18 0.23 rg 1 0 0 1 48.0 139.9 Tm (Not D: Forty does not equal 132 minus 84.) Tj ET
BT /F1 7.2 Tf 0.43 0.45 0.49 rg 1 0 0 1 48.0 126.5 Tm (Source: circulation-1:Q60 | 82) Tj ET
